"Matroid lifts and representability (https://www.combinatorics.org/ojs/index.php/eljc/article/download/v31i1p15/pdf/)\nciteturn2search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.8 years ago; In a recent paper, Walsh generalized this construction by defining a rank-\\(k\\) lift of a matroid \\(M\\) given a rank-\\(k\\) matroid \\(N\\) on the set of circuits of \\(M\\), and conjectured that all matroid lifts can be obtained in this way. In this sequel paper we simplify Walsh’s construction and show that this conjecture is true for representable matroids but is false in general.\nMatroid lifts and representability\n\nDaniel Irving Bernstein  \nMathematics Department  \nTulane University  \nNew Orleans, U.S.A.  \ndbernstein1@tulane.edu\n\nZach Walsh  \nSchool of Mathematics  \nGeorgia Institute of Technology  \nAtlanta, U.S.A.  \nzwalsh6@gatech.edu\n\nSubmitted: Jun 21, 2023; Accepted: Dec 20, 2023; Published: Jan 12, 2024  \n© The authors. Released under the CC BY-ND license (International 4.0).\n\n**Abstract**\n\nA 1965 result of Crapo shows that every elementary lift of a matroid \\(M\\) can be constructed from a linear class of circuits of \\(M\\). In a recent paper, Walsh generalized this construction by defining a rank-\\(k\\) lift of a matroid \\(M\\) given a rank-\\(k\\) matroid \\(N\\) on the set of circuits of \\(M\\), and conjectured that all matroid lifts can be obtained in this way. In this sequel paper we simplify Walsh’s construction and show that this conjecture is true for representable matroids but is false in general. This gives a new way to certify that a particular matroid is non-representable, which we use to construct new classes of non-representable matroids.\n\nWalsh also applied the new matroid lift construction to gain graphs over the additive group of a non-prime finite field, generalizing a construction of Zaslavsky for these special groups. He conjectured that this construction is possible on three or more vertices only for the additive group of a non-prime finite field. We show that this conjecture holds for four or more vertices, but fails for exactly three.\n\n**Mathematics Subject Classifications:** 05B35\n\n# 1 Introduction and preliminaries\n\nGiven matroids \\(M\\) and \\(L\\) on a common ground set \\(E\\), \\(L\\) is a lift of \\(M\\) if there exists a matroid \\(K\\) on ground set \\(E \\cup F\\) such that \\(M = K/F\\) and \\(L = K \\setminus F\\). If \\(L\\) is a lift of \\(M\\), then the rank of \\(L\\) is at least the rank of \\(M\\). We say that \\(L\\) is a rank-\\(k\\) lift of \\(M\\) if the rank of \\(L\\) is \\(k\\) greater than that of \\(M\\). Rank-1 lifts, called elementary lifts, are well understood. Indeed, a classical theorem of Brylawski [2], which was previously stated in the dual by Crapo [3], says that the elementary lifts of a matroid \\(M\\) are in bijection with the set of linear classes of circuits of \\(M\\), where a linear class is a set \\(\\mathcal{C}\\) of circuits satisfying the following:\n\nTHE ELECTRONIC JOURNAL OF COMBINATORICS 31(1) (2024), #P1.15  \nhttps://doi.org/10.37236/12154--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nRobust Connectivity of Graphs on Surfaces 851 (https://real.mtak.hu/130866/1/2021_Book_ExtendedAbstractsEuroComb2021.pdf)\nciteturn2search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5.2 years ago; For planar triangulations, we formulate the following conjecture. ... Hence, we propose the notion of robust connectivity as another way to attack Conjecture 1.\nRobust Connectivity of Graphs on Surfaces 851\n\nOur Results. We present asymptotically tight lower bounds for the robust connectivity of r-connected graphs in terms of their Euler genus, for r ≥ 3.\n\nTheorem 2. If r ≥ 3 and G is an r-connected graph of Euler genus γ, then  \nκρ(G) ≥ 1/27 γ^−1/r.\n\nThe Levi graphs of K_n^(r) show that this bound is tight within a constant factor. With more careful calculations, we also derive improved lower bounds for the robust connectivity of 3-connected planar graphs.\n\nTheorem 3. If G is a 3-connected planar graph, then κρ(G) ≥ 21/256 > 1/13.  \nMoreover, if ε > 0, then there exists a planar 3-connected graph H such that κρ(H) ≤ 1/3 + ε.\n\nIn this direction, we may attempt to go even further and exchange the assumption of 3-connectivity with being a planar triangulation. Note that planar triangulations on at least 4 vertices are 3-connected. For planar triangulations, we formulate the following conjecture.\n\nConjecture 2. If G is a planar triangulation, then κρ(G) ≥ 1/2.\n\nSurprisingly, Conjecture 2 turns out to be equivalent to the famous Conjecture 1.\n\nTheorem 4. Conjecture 1 is equivalent to Conjecture 2.\n\nHence, we propose the notion of robust connectivity as another way to attack Conjecture 1. In fact, we will present a further generalization of both conjectures to graphs of arbitrary Euler genus γ. In order to do this, we develop a new notion for an arbitrary surface S that, informally, describes how large of an induced subgraph we can cut out of an edge-maximal graph on S without separating S into multiple pieces. We will often write Ĝ to refer to an embedding of graph G. For a surface S, we let G_S be the family of (simple) embedded graphs on S. A graph G is edge-maximal (with respect to a surface S) if G has an embedding Ĝ ∈ G_S, but for each non-edge e ∉ E(G), G + e cannot be embedded in S. More often, we will speak about an edge-maximal embedding Ĝ ∈ G_S of graph G if for each e ∉ E(G), e cannot be added to the embedding Ĝ without creating crossing on S. Note that every edge-maximal graph G with respect to a surface S has an edge-maximal embedding on S, but not every graph with an edge-maximal embedding on S is edge-maximal with respect to S. For an embedded graph Ĝ ∈ G_S, we write S \\ Ĝ for the surface obtained by cutting S along the edges of Ĝ and puncturing S at each isolated vertex of Ĝ. Then, we define m(Ĝ) to be the number of vertices in a largest induced embedded subgraph Ĝ′ ⊆ Ĝ for which S \\ Ĝ′ is a connected surface. Now, we define the main parameter of interest.\n\nDefinition 2 (Surface connectivity). The surface connectivity κ_S(S) of a surface S is defined as follows: κ_S(S) = inf { m(Ĝ)/|G| : Ĝ ∈ G_S }.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMatroid lifts and representability (https://arxiv.org/abs/2306.12543)\nciteturn2academia14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3.4 years ago; In a recent paper, Walsh generalized this construction by defining a rank-$k$ lift of a matroid $M$ given a rank-$k$ matroid $N$ on the set of circuits of $M$, and conjectured that all matroid lifts can be obtained in this way. In this sequel paper we simplify Walsh's construction and show that this conjecture is true for representable matroids but is false in general.\nTitle: Matroid lifts and representability\nAuthors: Daniel Irving Bernstein, Zach Walsh\nDate: Wed Jun 21 20:16:12 2023\n\nA 1965 result of Crapo shows that every elementary lift of a matroid $M$ can be constructed from a linear class of circuits of $M$. In a recent paper, Walsh generalized this construction by defining a rank-$k$ lift of a matroid $M$ given a rank-$k$ matroid $N$ on the set of circuits of $M$, and conjectured that all matroid lifts can be obtained in this way. In this sequel paper we simplify Walsh's construction and show that this conjecture is true for representable matroids but is false in general. This gives a new way to certify that a particular matroid is non-representable, which we use to construct new classes of non-representable matroids. Walsh also applied the new matroid lift construction to gain graphs over the additive group of a non-prime finite field, generalizing a construction of Zaslavsky for these special groups. He conjectured that this construction is possible on three or more vertices only for the additive group of a non-prime finite field. We show that this conjecture holds for four or more vertices, but fails for exactly three.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMatroid lifts and representability - Science Explorer Abstract (https://scixplorer.org/abs/2023arXiv230612543B/abstract)\nciteturn2search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3.4 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; In a recent paper, Walsh generalized this construction by defining a rank-$k$ lift of a matroid $M$ given a rank-$k$ matroid $N$ on the set of circuits of $M$, and conjectured that all matroid lifts can be obtained in this way. In this sequel paper we simplify Walsh's construction and show that this conjecture is true for representable matroids but is false in general.\n\n## Matroid lifts and representability\n\n### Authors\n\nBernstein, Daniel Irving;\n\nWalsh, Zach\n\nEprint\n\nFull Text Sources\n\nOther Resources\n\n### Abstract\n\nA 1965 result of Crapo shows that every elementary lift of a matroid $M$ can be constructed from a linear class of circuits of $M$. In a recent paper, Walsh generalized this construction by defining a rank-$k$ lift of a matroid $M$ given a rank-$k$ matroid $N$ on the set of circuits of $M$, and conjectured that all matroid lifts can be obtained in this way. In this sequel paper we simplify Walsh's construction and show that this conjecture is true for representable matroids but is false in general. This gives a new way to certify that a particular matroid is non-representable, which we use to construct new classes of non-representable matroids. Walsh also applied the new matroid lift construction to gain graphs over the additive group of a non-prime finite field, generalizing a construction of Zaslavsky for these special groups. He conjectured that this construction is possible on three or more vertices only for the additive group of a non-prime finite field. We show that this conjecture holds for four or more vertices, but fails for exactly three.\n\n### Details\n\nPublication  | eprint arXiv:2306.12543\nPublication Date  | June 2023\nDOI  | 10.48550/arXiv.2306.12543\narXiv  | arXiv:2306.12543\nBibcode  | 2023arXiv230612543B\nCollection  | general\nKeyword  | Mathematics - Combinatorics\nE-Print Comment(s)  | The Electronic Journal of Combinatorics (2024): P1-15; doi:10.37236/12154--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n64  SIAM Conference on Applied Algebraic Geometry (AG25) (https://www.siam.org/media/13rgukxr/ag25_abstracts.pdf)\nciteturn2search15 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.4 years ago; Matroid Lifts and Representability ... This talk will discuss how a lift of M can be constructed via a matroid whose ground set is the circuit-set of M, and how this construction can be used to derive a combinatorial test for matroid representability. ... Zach Walsh\n64  SIAM Conference on Applied Algebraic Geometry (AG25)\n\nand Michael Joswig.\n\nLena Weis  \nTU Berlin  \nweis@math.tu-berlin.de\n\nGeorge Balla  \nMPI Leipzig  \ngeorge.balla@mis.mpg.de\n\nMichael Joswig  \nTechnische Universität Berlin, Germany  \njoswig@math.tu-berlin.de\n\nMS88  \nMatroid Lifts and Representability\n\nGiven a pair of matroids M and L on the same ground set E, one says that L is a lift of M if every flat of L is a flat of M. This happens, for example, when L is the column-matroid of a matrix A, and M is the matroid of a row-submatrix of A. This talk will discuss how a lift of M can be constructed via a matroid whose ground set is the circuit-set of M, and how this construction can be used to derive a combinatorial test for matroid representability.\n\nDaniel Bernstein  \nTulane University  \ndbernstein1@tulane.edu\n\nZach Walsh  \nAuburn University  \nzww0009@auburn.edu\n\nMS88  \nApplications of the 1-Dimensional Group Construction\n\nThe 1-dimensional group construction provides a flexible tool for constructing algebraic matroids. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\ncombinatorial theory 6 (1) (2026), #14 (https://escholarship